WEBVTT

00:02.600 --> 00:05.900
"It seems today that all you see

00:06.000 --> 00:09.200
"is violence in movies and sex on TV

00:09.300 --> 00:12.900
"But where are those good,
old-fashioned values

00:13.100 --> 00:15.000
"on which we used to rely?

00:16.100 --> 00:18.800
"Lucky there's a family guy

00:19.300 --> 00:22.500
"Lucky there's a man who'll
positively tell you

00:22.600 --> 00:24.200
"all the things that make us

00:24.300 --> 00:25.700
"laugh 'n' cry

00:26.000 --> 00:30.700
"He's a family guy"

00:33.300 --> 00:36.500
Mother, this hot dog has been
on my plate for a full minute...

00:36.600 --> 00:38.300
...and it hasn't yet cut itself.

00:38.400 --> 00:40.000
Honey, I'll be right there.

00:40.100 --> 00:42.100
By all means, take your time.

00:42.300 --> 00:45.700
When you do finally get around to it,
I'll be the one covered in flies...

00:45.800 --> 00:48.500
...with a belly that protrudes
halfway to bloody Boston!

00:48.600 --> 00:51.700
Mom, there is no way I'm sleeping
in Chris' room this weekend!

00:51.800 --> 00:53.300
It smells like old milk in there!

00:53.400 --> 00:55.500
If I could find it, I'd clean it up!

00:55.600 --> 00:57.100
Kids, keep it down.

00:57.200 --> 01:01.400
I haven't even told your father
that Aunt Marguerite is coming to visit.

01:01.500 --> 01:04.100
Who said Marguerite?
Peter, it's just for a week.

01:04.200 --> 01:08.400
A week! No. Please, God, kill me now.

01:08.600 --> 01:10.400
Damn, crap, damn it to hell, son of a...

01:10.500 --> 01:11.500
Peter!

01:11.600 --> 01:13.800
Lois, sometimes it's appropriate to swear.

01:13.900 --> 01:15.600
Do you swear to tell the whole truth...

01:15.700 --> 01:17.700
...and nothing but the truth,
so help you God?

01:17.800 --> 01:20.600
I do. You bastard.

01:20.900 --> 01:22.600
I love Aunt Marguerite.

01:22.700 --> 01:26.200
Because if it wasn't for her,
I never would've met you, Peter.

01:26.400 --> 01:28.800
Aunt Marguerite,
have you seen my towel?

01:28.900 --> 01:31.200
Have the towel boy bring you another.

01:31.300 --> 01:32.800
I don't want to bother him.

01:32.900 --> 01:36.600
Nonsense, dear. You're a Pewterschmidt.
Towel boy!

01:43.100 --> 01:46.400
Hi, my name is towel.
I have a Peter for you.

01:47.500 --> 01:49.900
My name is Peter.
I'll be your nipples... Towel boy!

01:50.000 --> 01:51.000
Oh, jeez.

01:53.000 --> 01:54.000
Okay, everyone.

01:54.100 --> 01:57.300
Give Aunt Marguerite
a big Griffin family welcome.

01:58.700 --> 02:00.100
Aunt Marguerite!

02:00.200 --> 02:01.200
Lois!

02:02.800 --> 02:05.600
Oh, my God! She's dead!

02:06.000 --> 02:09.200
Whoa!
Be careful what you wish for, huh, Lois?

02:10.500 --> 02:14.600
What if they bury her and she
wakes up because she wasn't really dead...

02:14.700 --> 02:15.900
...she was only sleeping?

02:16.000 --> 02:18.600
Yeah. That's what happened
to our big brother Jimmy.

02:18.700 --> 02:20.500
That's why Mom and Dad adopted you.

02:20.800 --> 02:21.700
What?

02:21.900 --> 02:24.900
Peter, you remember Coco,
my friend from Newport?

02:25.100 --> 02:28.500
Peter, I almost didn't recognize you
without a towel on your arm.

02:29.900 --> 02:33.100
Lois, where are your parents?
Don't tell me they're still on safari.

02:33.200 --> 02:34.200
You know Daddy.

02:34.300 --> 02:37.100
He won't rest until he kills something
on every continent.

02:37.900 --> 02:40.500
But I'm hoping they'll be back
in time for Christmas.

02:40.600 --> 02:42.800
It just wouldn't be Christmas
without your parents.

02:47.200 --> 02:48.600
I dropped my watch.

02:48.700 --> 02:50.500
Would you be a sport and fetch it for me?

02:50.600 --> 02:52.100
Sure thing, Mr. Pewterschmidt.

02:56.700 --> 02:58.400
Peter, we've got to put that out!

03:01.200 --> 03:03.000
I'm telling you, Brian, nothing changes.

03:03.100 --> 03:06.200
These bluebeards still treat me like scum
'cause I'm not loaded.

03:06.300 --> 03:09.500
I got news for them.
I'm as elegant as anyone in this room.

03:09.600 --> 03:12.500
We have to meet
with Aunt Marguerite's lawyer tomorrow.

03:12.600 --> 03:14.500
She left us something in her will.

03:14.700 --> 03:17.800
Holy crap!
You sweet old broad, I love you!

03:22.400 --> 03:24.300
Oh, my God. She's dead.

03:26.300 --> 03:28.800
Madam Pewterschmidt's passing
has saddened us all.

03:29.000 --> 03:30.500
Yeah, it's a real tragedy.

03:30.600 --> 03:33.400
What did we get? Come on, big money!
No whammy! Stop!

03:33.500 --> 03:37.600
Peter, please! I'm sorry.
He's stricken with grief.

03:37.700 --> 03:41.000
Before she passed,
your aunt recorded a message for you.

03:41.100 --> 03:42.600
Newport, Rhode Island...

03:42.700 --> 03:45.800
...home of New England's
most elegant and historic estates...

03:45.900 --> 03:50.100
...the Breakers, Rosecliff,
and exquisite Cherrywood Manor...

03:50.200 --> 03:52.700
...the palatial mansion
of Marguerite Pewterschmidt.

03:52.800 --> 03:56.100
Marguerite is a shining example of how
people with a lot of money...

03:56.200 --> 03:58.500
...are just plain better than everyone else.

03:58.700 --> 04:01.100
Lois, you were always
my favorite niece.

04:01.300 --> 04:03.500
I just knew you'd find a wonderful man...

04:03.700 --> 04:05.900
...who would make
all your dreams come true.

04:06.000 --> 04:07.000
But I was wrong.

04:07.400 --> 04:10.200
And now you're dead. Score one for Peter.

04:10.600 --> 04:14.000
It's time you started living
like a Pewterschmidt.

04:14.100 --> 04:17.500
That's why I'm giving you
my summer home in Newport.

04:17.800 --> 04:21.500
Cherrywood?
That's so generous of Aunt Marguerite.

04:21.800 --> 04:23.100
Our own summerhouse!

04:23.300 --> 04:26.000
Now I feel bad for doing that thing
with her toothbrush.

04:33.000 --> 04:36.000
"We only live to kiss your ass"

04:36.200 --> 04:38.300
Kiss it? We'll even wipe it for you.

04:39.300 --> 04:42.300
"From here on in, it's Easy Street"

04:42.500 --> 04:44.700
- Any bars on that street?
- 24 happy hours a day.

04:44.800 --> 04:45.700
Oh, boy.

04:45.800 --> 04:48.900
"We'll stop Jehovahs at the gate"

04:49.000 --> 04:50.900
Can I see that pamphlet, sir?

04:52.000 --> 04:55.500
"My God, this house is freakin' sweet

05:01.100 --> 05:04.000
"I make brunch, Clive cooks lunch

05:04.100 --> 05:07.300
"each and every day

05:07.400 --> 05:10.400
"Chocolate cake а la Blake

05:10.600 --> 05:14.000
"Hundred bucks, Blake is gay

05:17.300 --> 05:20.300
"We'll do the best we can with Meg"

05:20.400 --> 05:21.400
Are you saying I'm ugly?

05:21.500 --> 05:23.500
It doesn't matter, dear. You're rich now.

05:23.600 --> 05:26.700
"We'll do your nails and rub your feet"

05:27.100 --> 05:29.800
That's not necessary. Oh, my.

05:29.900 --> 05:32.800
"We'll do your homework every night"

05:32.900 --> 05:33.900
It's really hard.

05:34.000 --> 05:35.800
That's why we got
that Steven Hawking guy.

05:35.900 --> 05:39.800
"My God, this house is freakin' sweet

05:45.300 --> 05:48.300
"I used to pass lots of gas

05:48.500 --> 05:51.400
"Lois ran away

05:51.500 --> 05:54.700
"Now we've got 30 rooms

05:54.800 --> 05:57.900
"Hello, beans, good-bye, spray

06:01.500 --> 06:04.700
"We'd take a bullet just for you"

06:04.900 --> 06:06.600
What a coincidence. I've got one.

06:06.700 --> 06:07.800
Stewie!

06:07.900 --> 06:12.300
"Prepare to suck that golden teat

06:12.400 --> 06:15.600
"Now that you're stinking rich

06:15.800 --> 06:18.800
"we'll gladly be your bitch

06:19.000 --> 06:21.600
"My God, this house is

06:21.700 --> 06:24.900
"freakin' sweet"

06:25.600 --> 06:26.600
Welcome!

06:26.700 --> 06:29.100
That's a wrap, people.
Let's get the hell out of here.

06:29.200 --> 06:30.400
Wait. Where are you going?

06:30.500 --> 06:32.700
The old bag only paid us up
through the song.

06:32.800 --> 06:35.200
We can just pick up after ourselves.

06:35.300 --> 06:37.700
After all, we'll only be here on weekends.

06:37.800 --> 06:38.800
No, Lois.

06:38.900 --> 06:42.000
It's time you started living
like the Piece of Schmidt you are.

06:42.100 --> 06:43.600
That's "Pewterschmidt"!

06:43.700 --> 06:45.200
Wait, you guys.

06:45.300 --> 06:47.800
You're all hired to be
full-time Griffin servants.

06:47.900 --> 06:51.200
Peter, where are we gonna get the money
to pay all these people?

06:51.300 --> 06:53.500
Simple. I sold our house in Quahog.

06:53.600 --> 06:55.100
You sold our home?

06:55.200 --> 06:56.200
Surprise!

06:56.300 --> 06:57.600
Peter, how could you?

06:57.700 --> 06:58.600
Whoops.

06:58.800 --> 07:01.800
"I recognize that tone

07:01.900 --> 07:05.200
"Tonight I sleep alone

07:05.300 --> 07:10.300
"But still, this house is freakin' sweet"

07:17.000 --> 07:20.600
Peter, how could you sell our house
in Quahog without even asking me?

07:20.700 --> 07:24.800
Honey, this is where you belong.
You deserve a big house and nice stuff.

07:25.000 --> 07:25.900
Like diamonds.

07:36.000 --> 07:39.700
But I love our old house.
You have to buy it back.

07:39.800 --> 07:41.700
It's too late. Our stuff is packed.

07:41.800 --> 07:43.000
It's on its way here.

07:43.100 --> 07:45.200
Come on, Lois,
you'll love living in Newport.

07:45.300 --> 07:48.200
Sure, this house is big,
but it's also very intimate.

07:48.300 --> 07:50.600
Intimate.

07:50.700 --> 07:52.500
So we're really gonna live here now?

07:53.700 --> 07:55.000
That's right, honey.

07:55.100 --> 07:56.400
I don't know, Peter.

07:56.500 --> 07:59.000
Please, Mom. Look, there's a pool.

07:59.100 --> 08:00.600
Yeah. And there's a chair.

08:01.500 --> 08:04.100
The solarium is at the far end
of the west wing.

08:04.200 --> 08:08.900
Come play with us, Stewie,
forever and ever and ever.

08:09.500 --> 08:13.400
Yes. All work and no play
makes Stewie a dull boy.

08:16.200 --> 08:19.800
Across the hall from the library
we have the billiard room.

08:19.900 --> 08:22.500
And here we have the lounge.

08:22.600 --> 08:25.100
Sweet Mary, mother of God! Jackpot!

08:26.100 --> 08:27.600
What can I get you, sir?

08:27.700 --> 08:31.700
We have 10 varieties of single malt scotch
and a wine cellar with over 10,000 bottles.

08:31.900 --> 08:32.900
Don't make me beg.

08:33.200 --> 08:36.000
I did love spending time here
when I was a kid.

08:36.100 --> 08:37.600
All right, Mom!

08:40.100 --> 08:43.800
Jonathan and I just returned
from sailing our yacht around the world.

08:43.900 --> 08:45.400
Funny sailing story.

08:45.500 --> 08:47.800
A guy's on his boat,
in the middle of the ocean...

08:47.900 --> 08:49.500
...and he sees a little black dog.

08:49.600 --> 08:52.800
This dog's been swimming for days,
and he stinks like a dead otter.

08:52.900 --> 08:54.800
Peter, maybe this isn't the place...

08:54.900 --> 08:56.200
Hang on, Lois.

08:56.300 --> 08:58.500
So the guy takes the dog into the vet.

08:58.600 --> 09:02.500
The freakin' vet tells him, get this,
"It's not a dog. It's a rat."

09:02.600 --> 09:05.300
A big, stinking Mexican rat. True story.

09:06.400 --> 09:08.100
Dad, that's just an urban legend.

09:08.200 --> 09:11.300
Hand to God.
I'm telling you, it was a huge freakin' rat.

09:11.400 --> 09:13.200
Five times as big as that guy's steak.

09:13.300 --> 09:16.900
Peter, that rat gets bigger
every time you tell this story.

09:17.000 --> 09:18.300
I got a million of them.

09:18.400 --> 09:22.800
Like the time my buddy's sister's boss
was drinking with a hooker in a Vegas bar.

09:22.900 --> 09:24.300
Bam! Woke up without his kidney.

09:27.000 --> 09:29.500
I can't believe they kicked me
out of the yacht club.

09:29.600 --> 09:32.100
I barely had time
to stuff Lois' salmon in my jacket.

09:32.200 --> 09:35.000
Face it, Peter. You have a knack
for saying the wrong thing.

09:35.200 --> 09:36.200
This sucks.

09:36.300 --> 09:39.800
Lois' friend "yacht boy" and
his lovely wife "Caca" invited us to some...

09:39.900 --> 09:43.600
...hoity-toity auction tomorrow afternoon.
I don't want to embarrass her again.

09:43.700 --> 09:46.100
You gotta help me.
Teach me how to be a gentleman.

09:46.200 --> 09:50.000
Peter, it's not really that hard.
Let's start with polite conversation.

09:50.300 --> 09:54.100
For example, "It's a pleasure to see you
again. Lovely weather we're having."

09:54.200 --> 09:55.100
Now you try.

09:55.200 --> 09:57.000
It's a pleasure to see you again.

09:57.100 --> 10:00.100
After Hogan's Heroes,
Bob Crane got his skull crushed in...

10:00.200 --> 10:02.700
...by a friend who videotaped him
having rough sex.

10:03.100 --> 10:04.100
How's that?

10:04.200 --> 10:06.500
Wow, perfect. My work is done.

10:06.600 --> 10:08.600
But just for the heck of it, let's try again.

10:09.800 --> 10:11.200
More coffee, madam?

10:11.300 --> 10:13.300
I can get that, Sebastian.

10:13.400 --> 10:17.000
To tell you the truth, we're all
a little uncomfortable being waited on.

10:17.100 --> 10:18.100
Cut my egg!

10:19.800 --> 10:20.900
Your eggs are cut, sir.

10:21.000 --> 10:22.000
Cut my milk!

10:22.100 --> 10:23.300
I can't, sir. It's liquid.

10:23.400 --> 10:25.100
Imbecile! Freeze it, then cut it!

10:25.200 --> 10:27.800
If you question me again,
I'll put you on diaper detail.

10:27.900 --> 10:29.800
And I promise,
I won't make it easy for you!

10:29.900 --> 10:34.100
Meg, you're gonna love Newport High.
It has a beautiful campus.

10:34.500 --> 10:38.000
Filled with beautiful people.
And I'm gonna bag me a rich one.

10:38.100 --> 10:40.600
Meg, that's a terrible thing to say.

10:40.700 --> 10:44.300
You should marry someone you love.
That's what I did.

10:44.400 --> 10:46.400
And he got us kicked out of the yacht club.

10:46.500 --> 10:49.500
You can't be mad at your father
for being himself.

10:50.100 --> 10:53.100
That's the reason I fell in love with him
in the first place.

10:53.800 --> 10:56.200
He was so different from everyone else.

10:58.700 --> 11:00.700
Coco,
the day I graduate from Harvard...

11:00.800 --> 11:04.500
...l'm gonna carry you off into the sunset
on a white horse.

11:04.600 --> 11:07.700
It better be a stretch horse
with leather seats and a chauffeur.

11:07.800 --> 11:09.600
Isn't she a bit of terrific?

11:49.700 --> 11:53.800
Kids, if you marry for love,
your life will be filled with its own riches.

11:54.000 --> 11:55.500
Money doesn't buy happiness.

11:55.600 --> 11:56.800
I beg to differ.

12:00.100 --> 12:02.000
You!
Bring me The Wall Street Journal!

12:02.800 --> 12:04.200
You two, fight to the death!

12:14.200 --> 12:17.600
Okay, Peter, I was hoping I wouldn't
have to resort to shock therapy...

12:17.700 --> 12:19.500
...but your progress has been...

12:20.000 --> 12:22.200
Who are we kidding?
You haven't made any progress.

12:22.300 --> 12:26.300
Now the left TV is tuned to Frasier.
The right TV has Ricki Lake.

12:26.400 --> 12:29.700
If you so much as glance at the right TV,
I'm giving you 10,000 volts.

12:29.800 --> 12:30.800
Got it.

12:30.900 --> 12:33.300
You're so corpulent
that when you sit around...

12:33.400 --> 12:35.500
...the magnificently appointed
Tuscan villa...

12:35.600 --> 12:38.400
...you sit around the magnificently
appointed Tuscan villa.

12:39.000 --> 12:40.900
This is the smartest show on TV.

12:41.000 --> 12:44.500
Yo, Ricki. That's my girlfriend.
She ain't supposed to be having no penis!

12:46.700 --> 12:51.000
Master Brian, do you really believe you can
pass him off as a gentleman at the auction?

12:51.100 --> 12:54.600
We've got a long road ahead.
But I've worked miracles before.

12:55.400 --> 12:57.900
And the Oscar goes to Marisa Tomei!

13:07.400 --> 13:09.500
Peter was supposed to meet us here
an hour ago.

13:09.600 --> 13:12.000
I hope he didn't change his mind.

13:12.400 --> 13:13.400
Maybe he's already here.

13:13.500 --> 13:17.200
Maybe he's fitting in so well, we can't
tell him from the other bluebloods.

13:17.300 --> 13:20.100
I don't think we have to worry about that.

13:22.700 --> 13:25.000
Lord Peter Lowenbrau Griffin the First.

13:29.300 --> 13:31.000
Play me down the stairs, boys.

13:34.800 --> 13:35.800
Good day.

13:36.200 --> 13:37.100
Enchantй.

13:37.700 --> 13:39.000
Pasta Fazul.

13:42.200 --> 13:43.600
Looking good, fellas.

13:45.400 --> 13:47.300
Brian, do you know anything about this?

13:47.400 --> 13:50.400
Lois, please. I'm just a dog. A stupid dog.

13:50.500 --> 13:52.900
Vodka stinger with a whiskey back.
And step on it!

13:55.000 --> 13:57.300
Peter, you're simply enchanting.

13:57.400 --> 13:59.900
You must join us tomorrow
for a game of baccarat.

14:00.000 --> 14:02.200
Right baccarat at you.

14:05.100 --> 14:06.800
Brian, what happened to Peter?

14:06.900 --> 14:11.200
He's not cramming hors d'oeuvres in his
mouth or asking anyone to pull his finger.

14:11.400 --> 14:13.400
That's not the man I married.

14:13.700 --> 14:16.500
So, I guess, technically,
that makes you available.

14:16.700 --> 14:17.700
What?

14:18.400 --> 14:20.100
Lighten up, toots. It's a party.

14:22.100 --> 14:24.700
Barkeep,
it's like the damn Sahara over here!

14:24.800 --> 14:25.800
How you doing, honey?

14:25.900 --> 14:28.100
Welcome
to the Historical Society Auction.

14:28.200 --> 14:31.300
Our first item is
a 17th-century gilded vessel.

14:31.500 --> 14:34.600
We'll start the bidding at $140,000.

14:34.800 --> 14:36.000
What a marvelous vessel.

14:36.200 --> 14:39.800
It would look smashing in Lois's crapper.
I mean "crapier".

14:40.000 --> 14:41.300
You are so right.

14:41.400 --> 14:45.000
Any woman would love to have that vase
adorn her crapier.

14:45.100 --> 14:46.300
Jonathan!

14:47.400 --> 14:50.200
$140,000.

14:50.300 --> 14:51.500
$150,000.

14:51.800 --> 14:53.400
Brian, that sounded like Peter.

14:53.500 --> 14:54.900
Come here, you!

14:56.800 --> 14:58.200
$160,000.

14:58.300 --> 14:59.300
$170,000.

14:59.400 --> 15:00.700
- $180,000.
- $190,000.

15:02.600 --> 15:05.400
$200,000.

15:07.300 --> 15:10.100
We have a new record
for the Historical Society!

15:10.300 --> 15:11.300
The vessel goes to...

15:11.500 --> 15:13.800
$100 million!

15:15.100 --> 15:20.000
To Mr. Peter Griffin
for an astonishing $100 million!

15:21.300 --> 15:24.700
"Money, money, money"

15:28.800 --> 15:31.700
Peter, we had no idea
you were such a philanthropist.

15:31.800 --> 15:33.600
It's a fabulous vase, Peter, darling.

15:33.700 --> 15:35.500
Do you collect objets d'art?

15:35.600 --> 15:39.300
If that's French for "Star Wars
collectors glasses," then si.

15:41.100 --> 15:44.700
Mr. Griffin, you're the most generous man
since Ted Turner.

15:45.000 --> 15:49.200
I'd like to announce I'm giving a gift
the whole world can appreciate.

15:49.300 --> 15:50.800
I've colorized the moon.

15:51.400 --> 15:53.800
Peter, you don't have $100 million!

15:54.200 --> 15:55.800
Of course I do, my dear.

15:55.900 --> 15:57.700
Now, would that be cash or check?

15:57.900 --> 15:59.600
Drop by Cherrywood this evening.

15:59.700 --> 16:03.800
I'll have the money wired to me
from my Swiss bank account.

16:04.000 --> 16:05.100
Very good, sir.

16:05.200 --> 16:07.000
You don't have a Swiss bank account!

16:07.400 --> 16:08.600
Right.

16:09.000 --> 16:12.000
My lawyer's advised me
to keep some of my assets a secret...

16:12.100 --> 16:13.500
...in case things don't work out.

16:13.600 --> 16:16.100
I'm going home. Where's Brian?

16:17.200 --> 16:20.800
I told this blonde inside I got a 500SL.
Can you help me out?

16:23.500 --> 16:25.600
I'm sorry. But I've made my decision.

16:25.700 --> 16:28.400
We're moving back to Quahog
as soon as we can get packed.

16:28.500 --> 16:30.700
Quahog, that one-horse town?

16:31.500 --> 16:32.500
Shut up. No, you shut up.

16:32.600 --> 16:34.900
No, you shut up. You're the one talking.

16:35.000 --> 16:37.400
There's no one else here.
Everybody just shut up!

16:37.900 --> 16:40.000
What's that? The wind!

16:40.400 --> 16:41.700
A pox on Quahog!

16:44.400 --> 16:45.400
Easy!

16:45.500 --> 16:49.900
If I ever go back to Quahog, it'll be just
so I can poke poor people with a stick!

16:51.500 --> 16:53.600
Bon Jovi, everyone.

16:54.000 --> 16:56.000
Now I remember why I left Newport!

16:56.100 --> 16:59.200
It changes people.
You kids have lost your values.

16:59.300 --> 17:02.800
You've lost your mind! And I don't
much care for Stewie's new friends.

17:03.400 --> 17:07.400
The Pacific Rim economy
is still a tad shaky for my taste.

17:07.500 --> 17:08.700
Stop it!

17:08.800 --> 17:11.500
You can't become a bloody
fiscal hermit crab...

17:11.600 --> 17:13.700
...every time the Nikkei undergoes
a correction!

17:13.800 --> 17:15.800
Asia's market has nowhere to go but up!

17:16.300 --> 17:17.300
Interesting.

17:18.200 --> 17:19.200
Indeed.

17:20.000 --> 17:22.400
I wish we'd never come here
in the first place.

17:24.600 --> 17:26.100
Go buy yourself some more money.

17:30.000 --> 17:31.500
Hey, old bean.

17:32.100 --> 17:34.400
What are you doing
with my Star Wars glass?

17:34.600 --> 17:35.800
Illustrating a point.

17:36.400 --> 17:39.300
Peter, when Han Solo took
the Millennium Falcon to Cloud City...

17:39.400 --> 17:40.800
...he saw that Lando Calrissian...

17:40.900 --> 17:43.500
...had turned control of the station
over to Darth Vader.

17:43.600 --> 17:45.300
Lando had forgotten who he was.

17:45.400 --> 17:47.700
It was only after Han was
encased in carbonite...

17:47.800 --> 17:50.000
...and taken by Boba Fett
to Jabba's palace...

17:50.100 --> 17:52.500
...that he was able
to see the error of his ways.

17:52.600 --> 17:54.000
Look inside yourself.

17:54.100 --> 17:56.700
You're not a Newport millionaire.
I created you.

17:56.800 --> 17:58.900
In a way, I am your father.

17:59.400 --> 18:02.300
That's not true! That's impossible.

18:02.400 --> 18:03.900
Damn it, Peter! Snap out of it!

18:04.100 --> 18:05.200
No!

18:06.200 --> 18:10.900
I just had the craziest dream
where I bought a $100 million vase.

18:11.200 --> 18:15.000
A Mr. Brandywine from
the Historical Society is at the front gate.

18:15.100 --> 18:16.700
He'll be here in half an hour.

18:17.100 --> 18:19.400
That wasn't a dream.
He's here for the money.

18:19.500 --> 18:21.000
Brian, I'm screwed.

18:21.100 --> 18:23.600
If I welsh on that debt,
I'm gonna prove to everyone...

18:23.700 --> 18:25.900
...that I'm not good enough for Lois.

18:26.000 --> 18:28.000
If I only had something
worth that much money.

18:28.200 --> 18:30.100
I never should've dropped
Joe Green's jersey.

18:32.100 --> 18:35.100
Good game, Mean Joe.
You want some of my Coke?

18:40.900 --> 18:43.600
Hey, kid. Catch.

18:44.800 --> 18:46.500
Wow. Thanks, Mean Joe.

18:52.200 --> 18:53.900
What about this house?

18:54.000 --> 18:56.000
I could give him the house and call it even.

18:56.100 --> 18:57.900
Cherrywood isn't worth $100 million.

18:58.000 --> 19:01.400
Brian, it's the Historical Society.
We just gotta convince him that...

19:01.500 --> 19:04.000
...$100 million worth of history
happened here.

19:05.800 --> 19:08.700
So you're saying that Jesus carved
his name into this mantle...

19:08.800 --> 19:11.000
...51 years before he was born.

19:11.100 --> 19:14.000
Yeah. He's Jesus. He can do anything.
And look over here.

19:14.400 --> 19:17.600
That's where the market crashed.
Mr. Griffin!

19:17.700 --> 19:21.600
You can't take a step in this house
without uncovering something historical.

19:22.900 --> 19:24.600
Wait a second.

19:24.800 --> 19:27.800
Could that be Harriet Tubman's
secret underground railroad?

19:27.900 --> 19:31.100
It is! Go, Freedom Train!

19:31.200 --> 19:32.300
I've seen enough.

19:32.400 --> 19:36.200
I happen to know that nothing of
historical significance ever occurred here.

19:36.400 --> 19:38.800
Please, have our money ready
by tomorrow. Good day!

19:38.900 --> 19:39.900
Wait!

19:40.000 --> 19:42.800
Look, this is where the Pilgrims
landed at Fraggle Rock!

19:47.000 --> 19:48.700
Excuse me, Lord Griffin.

19:48.800 --> 19:51.100
Your family is going back to Quahog.

19:51.200 --> 19:53.900
If you get tired of being a snob, look us up.

19:54.500 --> 19:58.000
Lord Griffin is dead.
It's just me, Peter the towel boy.

19:58.100 --> 20:01.800
Peter, you're back! Let's go home!

20:01.900 --> 20:03.600
We can't. I sold our home.

20:03.700 --> 20:05.800
Our beautiful home with the stolen cable...

20:05.900 --> 20:08.100
...and the man with the penis
for a light switch.

20:08.200 --> 20:09.700
We'll find another place.

20:09.900 --> 20:13.300
Your Aunt Marguerite is laughing
at me while she's burning in Hell...

20:13.500 --> 20:15.100
...may she rest in peace.

20:15.200 --> 20:17.200
She was right. Everyone was right.

20:17.300 --> 20:19.000
I'm not good enough for you.

20:19.100 --> 20:24.000
Peter, I don't care what anyone else thinks.
All that matters is that I love you.

20:24.800 --> 20:26.100
I love you, too, Lois.

20:33.800 --> 20:35.800
Lois, our problems are over!

20:37.700 --> 20:40.000
Our mansion is historical, all right.

20:40.100 --> 20:43.000
Cherrywood was America's
first presidential whorehouse!

20:43.500 --> 20:46.500
See, there's Lincoln, Grant, Robert E. Lee.

20:46.600 --> 20:47.500
Those are fake!

20:47.700 --> 20:51.500
They're real.
And, FYI, Lincoln had the jungle fever.

20:55.300 --> 20:59.000
So you see,
Chubby Franklin lived across the street.

20:59.200 --> 21:03.000
Chubby Franklin would always
make a face like this.

21:03.100 --> 21:07.200
Dad, you're not listening.
I have a serious problem.

21:07.300 --> 21:10.200
I got a girl pregnant. What do I do?

21:10.500 --> 21:15.800
And when we saw Chubby Franklin
make his face, we would all make this face.

21:23.000 --> 21:25.700
Oh, God!

21:25.800 --> 21:28.900
Dad, you never did tell us
how you got our house back.

21:29.300 --> 21:32.600
Simple. I just offered the people
I sold it to double what they paid.

21:32.700 --> 21:34.600
What? But how could you afford that?

21:35.000 --> 21:38.400
I kept one of those Lincoln pictures
and held a little auction of my own.

21:38.900 --> 21:42.800
Thanks to old Honest Abe we have our
house back and I learned a valuable lesson.

21:42.900 --> 21:46.300
It doesn't matter if your family
doesn't think I'm good enough for you.

21:46.400 --> 21:50.200
That's right, because all that's important
is that I love you.

21:50.400 --> 21:53.800
No, because your ancestors were nothing
but a bunch of pimps and whores.

21:53.801 --> 22:54.801
Thank you for watching «Family Guy» on
www.FOX-fan.ru